<Project>

  <!--
    CHAIN TO THE PARENT Directory.Build.targets WHEN THERE IS ONE.

    Note that Directory.Build.props beside this file does NOT chain, and should not be read as the
    precedent for this: it is the ROOT of the props lookup for most of this repository, and the
    only file here that chains anything is Broiler.JS/Directory.Build.props' own consumer,
    Broiler.JS/Broiler.JS/Directory.Build.props, which reaches exactly one level. The shape below
    is copied from a sibling component instead: Broiler.UI/Directory.Build.props does the same
    thing for props, down to capturing the path in a property first and for the same reason.

    MSBuild stops at the FIRST Directory.Build.targets it finds walking up from a project, and
    this is the only one in this repository, so it wins for every project here, including the
    ones in the Broiler.DateTime, Broiler.Regex and Broiler.Unicode submodules. Composed inside
    an aggregate repository, that meant the aggregate's Directory.Build.targets reached every
    other component and none of ours.

    WHAT THAT COST. Broiler.Browser decomposes configuration names it invents (Debug-Linux,
    Release-Windows, Debug-VM and so on) onto a base configuration, because the SDK keys
    DebugSymbols off `Configuration == 'Debug'` and Optimize off `Configuration == 'Release'` and
    recognises nothing else. A name neither file recognises compiles UNOPTIMISED and succeeds
    silently. The copy of that mapping in our own Directory.Build.props covers the four
    Linux/Windows names it was written for, and cannot know a name invented later: at
    `Release-VM` this engine's twenty-two projects answered `Optimize=''`. Chaining means we
    inherit each new name as the consumer adds it, instead of this file going stale once per
    configuration.

    AND ADDING THE NAMES TO THAT COPY WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN THE SAME FIX. Of the twenty-three
    projects an aggregate build takes from this tree, four (Broiler.DateTime and the three
    Unicode projects) resolve their nearest Directory.Build.props to a vendored copy inside
    their OWN nested submodule, which no edit here reaches. All twenty-three resolve their
    Directory.Build.targets to this file. That asymmetry is why the repair belongs in this file
    and is one edit rather than four across four repositories.

    STANDALONE BUILDS ARE UNAFFECTED. With no Directory.Build.targets above this repository,
    GetPathOfFileAbove returns empty, the Condition is false and nothing is imported. The search
    starts one directory ABOVE this file, so it cannot find this file and recurse.

    THE PATH IS RESOLVED INTO A PROPERTY FIRST, and that is not a style choice: MSBuild's
    condition parser cannot read the nested quotes of a two-argument property function inside a
    Condition attribute, and answers MSB4092 "unexpected token" rather than treating it as a
    string. A property assignment is not parsed that way, so the call belongs there. The
    arguments are in BACKTICKS for the same reason one step removed: they are the one string
    delimiter that never collides with the quoting around them, so the call survives being moved
    back into an attribute by someone tidying up.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <BroilerParentBuildTargets>$([MSBuild]::GetPathOfFileAbove(`Directory.Build.targets`, `$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../`))</BroilerParentBuildTargets>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="$(BroilerParentBuildTargets)" Condition="'$(BroilerParentBuildTargets)' != ''" />

  <!-- Persisted generator output is a diagnostics opt-in. This file is imported after
       project properties, so Configuration and TargetFramework are both available and
       parallel builds cannot share an output directory. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(BroilerPersistGeneratedFiles)' == 'true'">
    <EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
    <CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\obj\generated\$(Configuration)\$(TargetFramework)</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
